[Music]  shattered dreams for the future due to  the economic crisis that followed hot on  the heels of the pandemic many people  lack job prospects  that can put a strain on mental health  in mexico an online platform offers free  counselling  in ghana one woman successfully led her  company through the hard times and  managed to save more than just her own  job  [Music]  also one new scientific study suggests  that covert 19 vaccines can affect  menstruation  should pregnant women still get the shot  but first to colombia where one young  college graduate can't find any work  and she's not the only one  [Music]  amanda libertad walks the streets of  bogota like she used to as a student  because she currently has little else to  do she's among the 30 percent of young  colombians who haven't been able to find  work since the pandemic amanda scans the  internet and social media looking for  job offers she's exhausted pretty much  every avenue  [Music]  it's hard because you go through the  application process and they don't tell  you why you didn't get the job  i'm even applying for jobs i'm not  qualified for because i don't know what  else to do  they always want two years experience  and i only have six months  colombia's economy is still reeling from  the pandemic unemployment is at 11.3  percent while 58 of those working only  have a short-term contract or casual  labor  experts say young people are the worst  affected  job seekers with no work experience it's  really tough  those who do have some experience  already have a network of colleagues  young people don't have that they're  just starting to develop contacts  so the situation is really hard for them  the crisis is also starting to take a  mental and emotional toll on colombia's  unemployed youth  i have constant insomnia now  not just a few bad nights  i go to bed at 10 but only sleep till 3  a.m  and i get up at 6.  when i go to bed i'm thinking what am i  going to do but what can i do i have no  money no job and all i can think is  what's in store for me  when am i going to start my career  how will i earn an income  experts are alarmed by growing levels of  insomnia and anxiety among young people  they say it's an emergency that requires  urgent action  a study conducted by the world health  organization and local psychologists  showed that 70 of young colombians now  have difficulty sleeping  young people feel desperate and deeply  insecure when they think about their  future  they have no way of knowing with any  reasonable amount of certainty what the  immediate future holds  and they feel they have no power to  change that  so they're extremely  most young colombians feel they're  facing bigger challenges than those  experienced by their parents generation  i think i'll look back on my youth with  great sadness  your twenties are supposed to be so  fantastic  but i think i'll just remember how sad i  was  i remember me crying and being anxious  i thought my 20s would be awesome  it's a very bad time so we've just  finished studying in colombia and have  no work experience  [Music]  things are especially tough for young  people who don't get any support from  their family the pandemic has presented  colombia with a whole new set of  challenges  [Music]  in mexico anxiety disorders and  depression became more widespread during  the pandemic sandra vargas wanted to  help and founded apoyo  the initiative provides mental health  care that everyone can access for free  online  [Music]  at the end of march 2020 prior to any  lockdown measures in mexico  sandra vargas already predicted the  emotional stress the pandemic would  bring in its weight  the psychoanalyst responded by creating  apoyocovid or covid support  an online platform providing free  therapy  just fill out a form and get a list of  professionals available 24 hours a day  the immediacy and anonymity of the  platform were key to people overcoming  inhibitions about seeking psychological  help  because it's embarrassing and scary  in mexico the number of people  experiencing mental health problems  tripled during the pandemic  anxiety disorders affected up to half of  the population and almost a quarter of  all mexicans developed signs of  depression  pablo  who is in the program is one such case  often it was the pandemic that triggered  or worsened existing mental health  issues  it manifested itself in the form of  anxiety i started eating more and i  gained more weight i suffer from chronic  depression and had a major breakdown i  didn't feel like doing anything not even  going to the supermarket  [Music]  apollo covid quickly gained momentum  marco garcia is among its 90 volunteers  he feels the project is a good  opportunity to overcome prevailing  taboos in mexico concerning mental  health  here it's deeply culturally ingrained  that if you ask for emotional support  you obviously can't take care of your  own problems  collective stigmatization stops us from  asking for help it seems a weakness  so far the project's webinars have  helped some two thousand people  they've given individual sessions to  more than six hundred patients from  across mexico and also from other  countries  most of them contacted apoyocobid during  a time of crisis like monica cervera did  when she called from the mexican state  of oaxaca  the pandemic triggered the separation  from her husband  i was in crisis that day  i couldn't stop crying and i locked  myself in my room  my children were in the living room  it helped me a lot at the time apollo  covid really saved me because i felt so  lost  despite the increasing demand in the  last decade the mexican government has  allocated only two percent of its total  health budget to the treatment of mental  health issues  and most of it goes into the maintenance  of psychiatric hospitals  we're barely managing to deal with  diarrhea and flu we still have a long  way to go with mental disorders they're  not priorities  right after her training at the mexican  psychoanalytic association  sandra vargas specialized in crisis  management  she offered psychological support to  those affected by the 2017 earthquake in  mexico city  life is finally returning to normal in  mexico and the apollo corbett platform  is receiving fewer requests  but it serves to strengthen the network  of therapists who are interested in  reaching out to a population in need  it's a commitment that has to come about  in a very organic way  referring people to us who need low-cost  mental health care attention  have helped to improve mental health in  mexico during the pandemic and will  continue their work going forward  and now to a question that concerns  roughly half of the world's adult  population  does the covid vaccination have an  effect on menstruation according to a  new study the answer is yes  dw reporter hangshuen lee talks to one  of the study's authors dr catherine lee  of washington university in saint louis  [Music]  hi dr lee so according to your study how  did covet vaccination affect people's  menstruation  what were the major findings our  study took place um  as an online survey  and what we found in our survey um which  i should point out is not representative  of the general population because people  could opt into our survey but what we  found was around 40  of people experienced a heavier period  after one or both  of their covet vaccines  around 40 percent um had no difference  in the heaviness of their bleeding  and  the remainder of people  were about the same or maybe slightly  lighter how about people who do not  usually menstruate for example  post-menopausal women or people on  gender affirming hormones  we also asked  people who normally wouldn't have a  period for any number of reasons  including long-acting  reversible contraceptives  like iuds that suppress bleeding and  people who are on gender affirming  hormones a lot of those folks  noticed that they had breakthrough  bleeding and the third group that we  really focus on in this paper is the  experiences of post-menopausal people so  people who are at least 12 months  past normally having a period who  noticed that they had breakthrough  bleeding after getting their covet  vaccine so what is the mechanism causing  these changes  of course we have our hypotheses and we  what we think is happening is that the  vaccine  is making this big immune response  that's why the vaccine is so incredibly  effective this is involving a lot of  inflammatory processes as well  and we know that the uterus is already  an important immune organ it uses these  inflammatory processes as part of the  signaling for  growing and shedding every month  and so it's not unreasonable to think  that an immune response that involves a  lot of inflammatory processes  might disturb in some people  these inflammatory processes for a you  know a couple of cycles after the  vaccine and what it changes temporary or  ongoing  so we  have just closed a follow-up survey that  actually asks about this um that asks  how many periods or for how many months  people noticed any changes  we have not started to analyze that data  yet  but anecdotally what we suspect is that  for most people it's just two or three  periods that they're noticing this  i would suggest if anyone is very  worried they're bleeding a lot or it's  going on for much longer that they see  their doctor  um because there's probably something  else going on that is not just this  inflammatory response that we think is  happening for some people with the  vaccine thanks a lot  what would you like to know about covid  our science correspondent derek williams  has the lowdown on the latest research  and analysis just write to covert  producer dw.com  this week we received a question from  maria abdullia robledo  are there any risks for pregnant women  who get vaccinated  it's really great for a change to talk  about something where experts are in  such broad agreement  to start with let's maybe approach this  question from the flip side instead of  asking  whether there are risks to being  vaccinated when you're pregnant  let's ask what the risks are if you  aren't vaccinated and there all the  evidence is really pretty unequivocal  many studies have shown that if they  catch covid19 while they're pregnant  unvaccinated women are a lot more likely  to have severe outcomes  than vaccinated women are at this point  almost everyone agrees that getting the  disease and having none of the immunity  provided by vaccines  that that can pose a major threat to to  both mother and fetus that's why when it  comes to covet 19 most health care  authorities now view pregnant women as a  vulnerable group but are there threats  lurking in the background adverse  effects that maybe occur more frequently  or dangers  that's proven trickier to quantify  because  pregnancy is an inherently  risky proposition  even before the advent of covet vaccines  miscarriages for example occurred about  10 to 20 percent of the time all on  their own and women who knew that they  were pregnant  but all the data that we have so far  involving pregnancy and vaccination and  it's a lot  doesn't show an increase in that metric  like everyone  pregnant women can and do experience  mostly minor side effects after  receiving vaccines but  according to the world health  organization  many pregnant women all over the world  have now been vaccinated against sars  kobe 2 and there have been no worrisome  signs that it has affected either their  health or that of their babies in fact  the opposite could actually be true  there are some studies indicating that  the antibodies that pregnant women  produce after vaccination  that they not only help protect the  fetus from infection with the  coronavirus during the pregnancy  but also after birth  a fairly small one cited on the cdc  website for instance it tracked levels  of antibodies and infants  six months after they were born and it  found  that the children of women who'd been  vaccinated against covet 19 during their  pregnancy  that they appeared to be even better  protected from the disease than the  children of women who were infected with  covet 19 during their pregnancy many  questions remain open of course but the  advice has has really evolved and it's  now pretty unanimous  among health care authorities  if a pregnant woman  hasn't been vaccinated yet they say  then she should definitely take the step  [Music]  a lack of prospects in their own country  is driving people around the world to  look for work elsewhere after some time  in chile a young mother from venezuela  has been stranded in a town in northern  spain but here too almost a third of the  population is threatened by poverty  [Music]  liska sandoval never thought she would  have to queue to meet her and her baby's  basic needs nevertheless covet 19 left  this venezuelan mother without a job in  chile and forced her to migrate to spain  now she's one of the beneficiaries of an  aid organization there  i never thought of asking for help of  any kind  if there had not been a pandemic i  wouldn't have had to come to the cochina  economica for help  the kothina economica located in  santander in northern spain is a  charitable organization that meets the  basic needs of society's most vulnerable  among their services such as social  dining or a hygiene facility they  provide food to more than one thousand  families monthly  because of the pandemic more people come  to the commissary and the canteen to  meet their basic needs some people have  survived until now thanks to their  savings we have people who had a stable  economic situation but because of the  pandemic and its consequences they're  now in situations of exclusion  more than one million people have fallen  into poverty in the mediterranean  country a recent human rights watch  report denounces the administration's  failure to protect the most vulnerable  people working hospitality industry  single parents older people on state  pensions  really suffered during the pandemic as a  result of the economic shock those  people found themselves in a situation  where they desperately needed  state assistance through the social  security system and that assistance was  very very slow in coming and was very  difficult to reach  at the start of the pandemic the spanish  government like other countries expanded  existing unemployment support programs  but the furlough support left out people  working in the informal economy and did  not adequately cover those working  seasonally or paid in part under the  table  another essential step was fast tracking  the introduction of the minimum vital  income a social assistance program  introduced in may 2020 but it's only  reached nine percent of the people  living at risk of poverty  it's a basic very very basic low-level  social assistance scheme for people who  haven't contributed to  social security systems and what it does  is it gives a very modest actually a  tiny amount of financial assistance to  people between 450 to 1000 euros a month  what people found as they tried to apply  for support through the system was a  series of difficulties first in trying  to apply  in trying to document their need  in the way the system calculated their  level of need  it was a good system  at some level but it really failed on a  number of bureaucratic levels and in  terms of its implementation  spain is a relative latecomer among  european countries in having a  nationwide social assistance program the  experts agree it's closely linked to the  increase of those who live at risk of  falling into poverty  koldo kasla director of the human rights  center clinic at the university of essex  affirms that poor social protection is  spain's achilles heel  association espanola cuarta economia de  la horizonte  19 not only brought consequences for  people's health and pressure on the  health systems but also an economic  storm that has wreaked havoc on the  lives of the most vulnerable  now bold government actions are needed  to ensure a better and fairer outcome  in the midst of the pandemic a woman in  ghana has set up a training program for  women living on the poverty line without  any government support as fashion  designers they now have the chance to  make their own money a real success  story  [Music]  bintu baka lives in shokor  a deprived community in ghana's capital  accra  she's a trained fashion designer and had  been able to earn a good income from the  profession  [Music]  but then the pandemic hit and her  livelihood came under threat  losing a secure source of income was  challenging she says  so during the lockdown being home not  getting any income sure whatever you  saved is what you have to use and after  you consume all what are you going to do  next it means that  you and your family won't get anything  to eat and  you have to either beg someone for  eating food to eat chocolates of your  house or whatever your health issues and  other things so  it was it was not that easy  barca and other workers were sent home  in the middle of the pandemic after  their employer an ngo run fashion  business suffered a sales lump and a  sudden dip in funding  patricia wilkins is the founder of that  ngo which is called basics international  and promotes children's and women's  welfare  she faced the task of keeping baka and  others in their jobs  and keeping her organization from  shutting down  kovit obviously hit everyone  by surprise by a storm  it's not something any of us had maybe  lived in a lifetime of we heard about  you know so many pandemics that was so  many years ago but for us  it was a shock so nobody knew  what to do governments also had to  rely on studies and react so for us  i think we went through a panic mode  like everyone and we shut down  despite the financial pressures and  great anxiety patricia wilkins was able  to reopen the ngo after only a short  closure  many women were dependent on her support  and urgently needed food and shelter  back at work baca and her colleagues  expanded their fashion portfolio and  began to make face masks  they then ventured into making items  like cloth bags and pillowcases cells  improved dramatically  today the business is able to sustain  itself and its employees  i'm feeling okay i feel excited for the  fact that i didn't lose my job and it's  still  going on because this is where for me i  get my money to take care of myself  to  pay my rent pay other things  buy myself clothes and whatever i need  so to take care of my health as well  in ghana the pandemic hit women  particularly hard  92 percent of women who have a job are  in informal work  when an economic crisis caused that  sector to collapse there was limited  government support to keep people afloat  the ngo action aid ghana recently  conducted research into the impact of  kovid on the livelihoods of ghanaian  women  its findings showed that they remain  vulnerable  nationally women fall within the  informal sector now this is a sector  that does not have job security this is  a sector where happenings within the  country can cause a closure uh to a  business and so if you have  a a higher percentage of your population  uh being in that sector in their women  it clearly shows that once a pandemic  struck  they would definitely be the ones to  bear the brunt of this pandemic  but patricia wilkins is not giving up  every day she works hard to keep her  organization up and running without any  government support  bintu bacca and the other women here can  breathe a sigh of relief knowing that  for now their income is secure  and that's it for this week  next week we'll take a closer look at  what side effects covid19 vaccines can  have and whether in some exceptional  cases they might last longer than  expected  there are now years of research and  analysis that can be drawn on  reporter stephanie tsobel spoke with  klaus kichutek head of the powell erlich  institute to find out more  we look forward to seeing you next time  stay healthy  [Music]

